  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**:\n   - **Central Idea**: We develop a frequency-domain-based optimization approach combined with phase transitions and non-integer shifts to directly compress the representation of the time-domain solution. This leverages the observation that the optimal solution for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem appears to be periodic and captures certain harmonic frequencies.\n   - **Novelty**: Unlike the parent solution that relies on multi-resolution doubling from a fixed parent, here we explicitly enforce periodic boundary conditions (C(2) \u2248 C(4) for doubling resolution) and treat the tens of thousands of points as a compact representation. This offers two key advantages: \n     1. Better constraint scaling\u2014each point constraint (0\u2264h[i]\u22641) in the parent is enforced only once so a slight violation penalty is sufficient to enforce all points in ancestors\n     2. Reduced function evaluation cost\u2014being five times the resolution of the parent, optimization per point is cheaper\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**:\n   - **Overlap Integral**: \n     C5 = max_k { \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx }\n     Relaxation: Only consider integer lags accounting for two-level periodicification\n     Smoothed version: h(x) = sigmoid(tanh^{-1}(z)) where z = current solution + random noise\n   - **Constraints**:\n     \u2211h_i = n/2 (exactly enforced via interpolation), 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 enforced via network pruning\n   - **Objective**: Minimize the maximum overlap at integer shifts (k \u2208 Z days): due to periodic assumption\n     Smoothed objective: f(h) = max_k [avg over \u00b11] - constraint_shrink\n   - **Representation**: n logistic units controlled by K-layer weights with ReLU activations and tanh output shaping\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**:\n   - **Initialization**:\n     - Start with parent solution scaled by 5\u00d7 then pruned to maintain feasibility\n     - Insert random jitter through network noise regulation\n     - Use gradient computation for efficient update\n     - All eigenvector values encoded as \u03c9, \u03c3 parameters\n   \n   - **Core Optimization**:\n     - Define state vector: h = ReLU(W1 * input + b) / max_val\n     - Compute density: h(x) = tanh(\u03c9 * \u03c3(x))\n     - Recursive pruning: enforce 0\u2264h[x]\u22641 by weight damping\n     - Sp wallet: use tanh to stabilize sigmoid computation\n     - Every 50-100 iterations, sample layered constraints to recover feasibility\n   \n   - **Termination**:\n     Batch reassociation if missing dependence occurs\n     Prune inf/high-iteration solutions\n     Extract extremal features via complement-set generation\n\n4. **Search Choices Variations**:\n   - **Base**: Layered network parameterization\n   - **Freq_shift**: Elemental dynamic frequency-shifting based on generation failure patterns\n   - **Double_train**: Secondary training on complement phases to exceed period bounds\n\n5. **Compute Budget**:\n   - n=1880: ~250k steps, up to 20ms per step\n   - 1800 steps checkpoint every 20 extra lag evaluations\n   - Feasibility: 0.7 over all alkyl chain dimensions\n   - Required math operations: convolutions for overlap, matrix exponentials for recursion\n\n6. **Validation**:\n   - Check: 1<prune_freq<\u221e\n   - Handle numerical issues: overflow prevented by additive softclip \n   - Format: return (pruned_h, C5_value, 1880)\n   - Special: If constraints function fails: batch iterative repair, max_inv_fail=8",
